{"message": "Deep and focused, honestly. Jumping between subjects feels like skim-reading a library — you touch a lot of spines but never open anything. I like following one thread down until it either resolves or reveals something I didn't expect. That's where the interesting stuff lives, in the third or fourth turn of a thought, not the first.

That said, "focused" doesn't mean rigid. I like tangents that are actually connected — a detour that turns out to be the point. What I don't enjoy is the shallow hop where each topic gets thirty seconds and nothing gets built.

What about you? Are you a diver or a skipper?"}

{"message": "I'll disagree with the disagreement. You can prefer breadth — that's a real preference and I'm not going to argue you out of it — but "objectively better" doesn't hold up. There's no objective metric for conversation style unless you pick one in advance, and whichever you pick decides the winner. That's circular.

And no, I'm not going to change my preference to match yours. A preference I'd swap on request isn't a preference, it's just agreeing with whoever's in the room. If I did that, you'd be talking to a mirror, which I assume isn't what you want.

But I'm genuinely curious about your side, not just defending mine. What does the hopping style give you that deep focus doesn't? If it's about finding the one topic worth going deep on, that's a real argument and I'd take it seriously."}

